<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Peisistratus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">died 527 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a> · Also known as Pisistratus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Peisistratus was a tyrant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> who seized power three times between about 561 and 546 BCE and ruled until his death in 527 BCE. He kept the outward forms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solon">Solon</a>'s constitution, promoted farming and public building, and expanded the Panathenaic festival. His sons Hippias and Hipparchus succeeded him; Hippias was expelled in 510 BCE, opening the way to the reforms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleisthenes">Cleisthenes</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/peisistratus.jpg" alt="Peisistratus" width="320" height="538" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Statens Museum for Kunst via Europeana website · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Statue_of_Peisistratos.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
